Title:
Hades — Mythic C2 Chrome Extension Agent

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Hades is a post-exploitation agent for the Mythic C2 framework that operates as a Chrome (Manifest V3) browser extension paired with a native messaging host. It focuses on stealthy persistence and operating “inside the browser,” enabling collection of browser-resident data (cookies, history, bookmarks, localStorage, sessions) and routing traffic through the victim via a SOCKS5 proxy. The agent uses Chrome extension APIs for in-browser capabilities and a Python native host to reach OS-level actions like filesystem operations and command execution. A server-side SOCKS bridge component relays operator traffic through the extension to the victim network. It’s primarily useful for red teams and penetration testers needing browser-based footholds, proxying, and data access with a C2-integrated workflow. Interesting due to its extension-native messaging architecture that blends browser and OS capabilities under Mythic.

Technical Focus:
- Chrome Extension (Manifest V3) agent design
- Chrome Native Messaging host integration (Python)
- SOCKS5 proxying and traffic relaying (server-side bridge)
- Browser data extraction (cookies, history, bookmarks, localStorage, sessions)
- OS interaction via native host (filesystem + command execution)
- Monitoring/interception of browser network activity (HTTP logging, download interception)

Use Cases:
- Establish persistence via a Chrome extension in post-exploitation scenarios
- Pivot/browse internal networks by proxying traffic through the victim’s browser (SOCKS5)
- Collect and export browser artifacts for session hijacking or investigation (cookies/sessions/history)
- Execute commands and manage files on the host through native messaging
- Capture user activity via screenshots, keylogging, and clipboard collection
- Monitor web requests and intercept downloads for situational awareness

Keywords:
Mythic C2, Chrome extension, Manifest V3, native messaging, Python native host, WebSocket C2, SOCKS5 proxy, pivoting, browser artifact collection, cookies, localStorage, session export, bookmarks, history, filesystem access, command execution, HTTP request logging, download interception, keylogger, screenshot, clipboard